      "id": "IVR-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Two Hecke estimates recover the original stable periods",
      "anchor": "inputs-invariant-decay",
      "statement": "For the integrated cochain of a mean-zero remainder, each fixed cycle has Hecke-iterated periods bounded by C_z(3/5)^j. At a large enough size N the period vector x_j on the stabilized finite spanning family also satisfies (4/5)^j‖x_0‖≤‖x_j‖. Hence x_0=0 and all original periods vanish.",
      "hypotheses": "L′ is totally complex and the degree m=k+1 is positive and strictly smaller than r₂(L′). The prime element π has absolute ideal norm at least 25. The size N meets the fixed-size Hecke comparison, integrability and N≥3 thresholds. The remainder is continuous, has closed simplex cochain, is arithmetic- and center-invariant, and its frame coefficients are square-integrable of mean zero.",
      "definitions": [
        "normalized Hecke average",
        "finite spanning family at n=2m+4",
        "stabilization by identity blocks",
        "two Hecke coset indices",
        "period vector in the supremum norm"
      ],
