      "correspondence": "In size n finite transfer of double cosets makes the smaller Hecke action descend to the finite-dimensional period vectors. For α=I_n/I_N the transferred vector is (1−α)x_j+αy_j with ‖y_j‖≤K‖x_j‖. The index lower bound and α(1+K)≤1/5 give the lower bound on this finite vector at N. The upper bound is uniform on that vector because the spanning family is finite. Homological stability propagates x_0=0 to every large-size cycle.",
      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "review": "An independent mathematical reader verified the double-coset comparison, fixed-size bound K, large-size thresholds, the two rates on the same stabilized vector, the example 64/125 > 54/125, and passage from the finite spanning family to all periods."
    },
    {
      "id": "IVR-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Archimedean invariant forms represent stable real cocycle periods",
      "anchor": "inputs-invariant-rank",
      "statement": "For each initial matrix size, some N at least that large works for every real homogeneous arithmetic m-cocycle c at N. An archimedean-invariant m-form on the product of positive Hermitian cones then has the same periods as c on every arithmetic m-cycle at N. The selected odd-degree rank proof applies this equality to a separating cocycle and then bounds the image of filtered cycles by r₂.",
      "hypotheses": "The degree m is positive and smaller than the number of complex places of the totally complex number field. In the application m=2e+1 and the initial size of the filtered sequence is at least 2m+4. The real cocycle is invariant for simultaneous left translation of its m+1 arguments.",
      "definitions": [
        "homogeneous cochain",
        "affine Hermitian simplex",
        "normalized Haar mean of transported tangent coefficients",
        "period equality on cycles"
      ],
